# at — schedule a one-time task

## Basic usage

```sh
at {time}
# then type commands, one per line, finish with Ctrl-d
{command 0}
{command 1}
# Ctrl-d
```

## Time formats

```sh
now | midnight | noon | teatime   # teatime = 4pm
HH:MM
now + N minutes|hours|days|weeks
MM/DD/YY
```

## List pending jobs

```sh
atq
```

## Remove a job (use id from atq)

```sh
atrm {id}
```

## Real-world: one-shot reboot tonight after a kernel update

```sh
echo "systemctl reboot" | sudo at 02:30
```

## Real-world: start the coffee maker before an early morning

```sh
echo "python3 ~/scripts/coffee_on.py" | at 05:45
```

## Real-world: inspect what a queued job will run

```sh
at -c {id}
```

Note: the `atd` service must be running — `sudo systemctl enable --now atd`. For anything recurring, use a systemd timer or cron instead.

| Command | What it does           |
| ------- | ---------------------- |
| at      | queue a one-time job   |
| atq     | list queue             |
| atrm    | remove job             |
| at -c   | print job's script     |
